  },
  "same_as": []
 },
 "kind": "statement",
 "title": "Projective simplicial diagram models and change of index",
 "statement": "Fix universes $\\mathcal U\\in\\mathcal U'$; small means $\\mathcal U$-small and all categories under discussion fit in $\\mathcal U'$. Use simplicial model categories of [lem-coef-simplicial-model-nerve-localization], and simplicial categories and Dwyer--Kan equivalences of [lem-coef-coherent-nerve-comparison]. Let $\\mathcal A$ be a combinatorial simplicial model category: its underlying category is generated under $\\kappa$-filtered colimits, for some regular $\\kappa$, by a small set of objects whose Hom functors preserve those colimits, and its two model weak factorization systems are generated by small sets. For every small simplicial category $I$, the category $\\mathcal A^I$ of simplicial functors and enriched natural transformations has a combinatorial simplicial projective model structure, with objectwise weak equivalences and fibrations. Its simplicial tensor and cotensor are objectwise, and its mapping simplicial set is the enriched end of the objectwise mapping sets. Evaluation preserves cofibrations and trivial cofibrations, and in particular sends projectively cofibrant fibrant diagrams to cofibrant fibrant objects. The enriched free diagram left adjoint to evaluation at $i$ is $X\\mapsto I(i,-)\\otimes X$.\n\nIf $v:I\\to J$ is a Dwyer--Kan equivalence of small simplicial categories, enriched left Kan extension and restriction give a simplicial Quillen equivalence $v_!: \\mathcal A^I\\rightleftarrows\\mathcal A^J:v^*$. A Quillen equivalence here means that for cofibrant $P$ and fibrant $Q$, a map $v_!P\\to Q$ is a weak equivalence exactly when its adjoint $P\\to v^*Q$ is. No local Kan hypothesis on $I$ or $J$ is imposed.",
 "source": {
  "document": "https://www.math.ias.edu/~lurie/papers/HTT.pdf",
  "place": "Proposition A.3.3.2, Remarks A.3.3.4-A.3.3.5, Propositions A.3.3.7-A.3.3.8, printed pp.870-873, specialized to Kan-model simplicial sets. All simplicial sets are cofibrant, so the objectwise-cofibration assertion of Remark A.3.3.5 applies. Read online; not archived in literature/. No Stacks tag states this simplicial homotopy-theoretic result."
 },
 "stmt_deps": [
  "lem-coef-simplicial-model-nerve-localization",
  "lem-coef-coherent-nerve-comparison",
  "def-cbc-enhanced-diagrams-cones"
 ],
 "proof": [
  {
   "text": "Write $O=\\operatorname{Ob}I$ and $\\mathcal B=\\prod_{i\\in O}\\mathcal A$. The product is locally presentable by [lem-coef-accessible-monad-presentability]. On it define\n$$ (TX)_j=\\coprod_{i\\in O} I(i,j)\\otimes X_i. $$\nThe unit inserts the identity vertex in $I(j,j)$ and the multiplication uses $I(j,k)\\times I(i,j)\\to I(i,k)$ and the tensor associativity. Thus the monad identities are precisely the identity and associativity identities of $I$. A $T$-algebra is exactly a family $X_i$ with unital associative action maps $I(i,j)\\otimes X_i\\to X_j$, hence an enriched functor; algebra morphisms are exactly enriched natural transformations. For each simplicial set $K$, $K\\otimes-$ is left adjoint to $(-)^K$, by the tensor/cotensor axiom of [lem-coef-simplicial-model-nerve-localization]; it therefore preserves colimits (apply the adjunction to the defining cocone). Coproducts also commute with colimits by their universal property. Consequently $T$ preserves all small colimits, in particular filtered colimits. Apply the accessible-monad statement to deduce that $\\mathcal A^I$ is locally presentable, complete and cocomplete. Colimits in it are objectwise: the colimit of each action map exists since $I(i,j)\\otimes-$ preserves that colimit, and the action identities hold after every colimit insertion. Limits are objectwise as well: the action into each limiting value is the unique map with the prescribed projections, whose action identities follow after those projections. All coproduct and product indexing sets above are small.",
   "uses": [
    "lem-coef-accessible-monad-presentability",
    "lem-coef-simplicial-model-nerve-localization"
   ],
   "elementary": false,
   "source": {
    "document": "literature/blueprint/main-20261015/blueprint/sec-six-functors/sk-thm-sf-package/prop-coef-integral-exceptional-adjoint.proof.md",
    "place": "Proof paragraph 2, coherent derived coefficient-adjunction foundation; HTT A.3.3.2 and the projective part of A.2.8.2: presentability and pointwise colimits, expanded via an accessible monad"
   }
  },
  {
   "text": "For $i\\in O$ define $F_iX$ by $(F_iX)_j=I(i,j)\\otimes X$, with action given by composition. A transformation $F_iX\\to P$ is determined by its restriction $X\\to P_i$ at the identity of $i$; conversely a map $a:X\\to P_i$ gives the component $I(i,j)\\otimes X\\xrightarrow{1\\otimes a}I(i,j)\\otimes P_i\\to P_j$. The two constructions are inverse by the identity and composition axioms. For every $n$, tensor associativity identifies $(F_iX)\\otimes\\Delta^n$ with $F_i(X\\otimes\\Delta^n)$. The displayed inverse constructions therefore identify the $n$-simplices of $\\underline{\\operatorname{Map}}(F_iX,P)$ with maps $X\\otimes\\Delta^n\\to P_i$, the $n$-simplices of $\\underline{\\operatorname{Map}}(X,P_i)$. Restriction along each simplex operator commutes with inserting the identity and with the action maps, so these bijections give an isomorphism of simplicial sets, natural in $X,P$. This proves the enriched adjunction. In particular $F_i$ preserves all small colimits. Let $S$ and $T_0$ be small generating cofibrations and generating trivial cofibrations of $\\mathcal A$, and put $S_I=\\{F_i(s)\\}$, $T_I=\\{F_i(t)\\}$. A square against $F_i(s)$ or $F_i(t)$ is, under this adjunction, exactly a square against $s$ or $t$ at $i$, with the same choices of diagonal filler. Hence $S_I^{\\square}$ is exactly the class of objectwise trivial fibrations and $T_I^{\\square}$ exactly the class of objectwise fibrations. This uses only the specified two generating lifting systems and tensor adjunction of [lem-coef-simplicial-model-nerve-localization].",
   "uses": [
    "lem-coef-simplicial-model-nerve-localization"
   ],
   "elementary": false,
   "source": {
    "document": "literature/blueprint/main-20261015/blueprint/sec-six-functors/sk-thm-sf-package/prop-coef-integral-exceptional-adjoint.proof.md",
    "place": "Proof paragraph 2, coherent derived coefficient-adjunction foundation; HTT A.3.3.5 and A.2.8.2: free diagrams and the two right lifting classes"
   }
  },
  {
   "text": "For every domain $A$ of an arrow of $S_I\\cup T_I$, [lem-coef-object-presentability-rank] supplies a small infinite regular $\\tau_A$ for which $\\operatorname{Hom}(A,-)$ preserves $\\tau_A$-filtered colimits. Apply [lem-coef-small-family-regular-bound] to the small family of underlying sets of these cardinals; it gives an infinite regular $\\lambda$ larger than every $\\tau_A$. The ordinal $\\lambda$ is $\\tau_A$-filtered: fewer than $\\tau_A$ of its elements have an upper bound below $\\lambda$ by regularity. Thus each domain Hom functor preserves colimits of $\\lambda$-chains, including the continuous cellular chains required in [lem-coef-small-object-cell-factorization]. Apply that node separately to $S_I$ and $T_I$. It gives functorial cell-map/right-lifting factorizations and identifies each left lifting class with the arrow retracts of its cell maps. Write $C_I={}^{\\square}(S_I^{\\square})$ and $D_I={}^{\\square}(T_I^{\\square})$ for these classes. They form functorial weak factorization systems with the indicated right classes: a map right-orthogonal to the left class is right-orthogonal to its generators, and the converse follows by extending a lift over each cell and passing to retracts. The same lifting argument proves closure of either left class under small coproducts, pushouts and continuous transfinite compositions: solve on each summand, use the pushout universal property, and extend successively with the colimit lift at each limit stage. No choice of a functorial factorization is inferred from a merely existential cellular statement.",
   "uses": [
    "lem-coef-object-presentability-rank",
    "lem-coef-small-family-regular-bound",
    "lem-coef-small-object-cell-factorization"
   ],
   "elementary": false,
   "source": {
    "document": "literature/blueprint/main-20261015/blueprint/sec-six-functors/sk-thm-sf-package/prop-coef-integral-exceptional-adjoint.proof.md",
    "place": "Proof paragraph 2, coherent derived coefficient-adjunction foundation; HTT A.2.8.2(i)-(iii), used in A.3.3.2: both small-object factorizations and their explicit retract argument"
   }
  },
  {
   "text": "Every simplicial set is cofibrant in the Kan model structure [lem-coef-kan-quillen-right-proper]. The simplicial model axiom [lem-coef-simplicial-model-nerve-localization], applied to $\\varnothing\\to I(i,j)$, implies that $I(i,j)\\otimes-$ sends cofibrations to cofibrations and trivial cofibrations to trivial cofibrations: indeed, a lifting square from $a\\mathbin\\square b$ to a coefficient fibration $p$ is, by the tensor/mapping adjunction, a lifting square from the simplicial arrow $b$ to the mapping-space arrow associated to $(a,p)$. If $a,b$ are cofibrations, the latter square lifts when $p$ is trivial; if either $a$ or $b$ is trivial, it lifts for every fibration $p$. The two coefficient lifting systems give the claimed pushout-product classes. Therefore the generators $S_I$ evaluate to cofibrations and the generators $T_I$ to trivial cofibrations. Evaluation preserves colimits by Step 1. The lifting-extension and retract arguments of Step 3, now in $\\mathcal A$, show that every $C_I$ map is objectwise a cofibration and every $D_I$ map is objectwise a trivial cofibration. In particular $D_I\\subseteq W_I$, where $W_I$ means objectwise weak equivalence. Since $S_I^{\\square}$ is contained in $T_I^{\\square}$, one also has $D_I\\subseteq C_I$.",
   "uses": [
    "lem-coef-kan-quillen-right-proper",
    "lem-coef-simplicial-model-nerve-localization"
   ],
   "elementary": false,
   "source": {
    "document": "literature/blueprint/main-20261015/blueprint/sec-six-functors/sk-thm-sf-package/prop-coef-integral-exceptional-adjoint.proof.md",
    "place": "Proof paragraph 2, coherent derived coefficient-adjunction foundation; HTT A.3.3.5: local cofibrancy of the indexing category and evaluation of the cellular generators"
   }
  },
  {
   "text": "The classes $W_I$ are retract closed and satisfy two-out-of-three, object by object, by the model axioms of [lem-coef-simplicial-model-nerve-localization]. If $f\\in C_I\\cap W_I$, factor it using Step 3 as $f=pj$, with $j\\in D_I$ and $p$ objectwise a fibration. Step 4 gives $j\\in W_I$, so $p\\in W_I$ by two-out-of-three. Thus $p\\in S_I^{\\square}$ and the left lifting property of $f$ makes $f$ an arrow retract of $j$. It follows that $f\\in D_I$. We have proved $D_I=C_I\\cap W_I$, while $S_I^{\\square}=T_I^{\\square}\\cap W_I$ is the identity already proved in Step 2. The two weak factorization systems of Step 3 are consequently exactly $(C_I,F_I\\cap W_I)$ and $(C_I\\cap W_I,F_I)$ with $F_I$ the objectwise fibrations. Completeness and cocompleteness were proved in Step 1. These are all the model axioms, with functorial factorizations generated by the two small displayed sets; together with local presentability they prove combinatoriality. This argument does not require cofibrant domains for the chosen generating maps.",
   "uses": [
    "lem-coef-simplicial-model-nerve-localization"
   ],
   "elementary": false,
   "source": {
    "document": "literature/blueprint/main-20261015/blueprint/sec-six-functors/sk-thm-sf-package/prop-coef-integral-exceptional-adjoint.proof.md",
    "place": "Proof paragraph 2, coherent derived coefficient-adjunction foundation; HTT A.2.8.2 projective lifting argument, as invoked in A.3.3.2"
   }
  },
  {
   "text": "Define the tensor and cotensor of diagrams pointwise, with action maps induced by tensor associativity and by its adjunction, respectively. Their mapping simplicial set is the equalizer\n$$\\prod_i\\underline{\\operatorname{Map}}(P_i,Q_i)\\rightrightarrows\\prod_{i,j}\\underline{\\operatorname{Map}}_{\\mathsf{sSet}}\\bigl(I(i,j),\\underline{\\operatorname{Map}}(P_i,Q_j)\\bigr).$$\nThe two arrows compose the family of maps with the actions of $P$ and $Q$. A map from a parameter $K$ to this equalizer is exactly a family $K\\otimes P_i\\to Q_i$ satisfying enriched naturality, which verifies the asserted tensor and cotensor adjunctions and the enriched-end description. For a generating arrow $F_i(a)$ in either $S_I$ or $T_I$ and a simplicial cofibration $b$, the pushout product is $F_i(a\\mathbin\\square b)$, since $F_i$ commutes with tensors and colimits. The simplicial model axiom in $\\mathcal A$ makes $a\\mathbin\\square b$ a cofibration, trivial if $a$ or $b$ is trivial; $F_i$ preserves these two classes by Step 2's lifting adjunction. For fixed $b$, pushout product preserves pushouts, coproducts and continuous transfinite composites of arrows, as follows by writing each side as its defining colimit, and it preserves retracts by functoriality. Applying Step 3 to $S_I$ therefore proves that $c\\mathbin\\square b$ is a cofibration for every projective cofibration $c$, and is trivial when $b$ is trivial. Applying Step 3 separately to $T_I$ proves that $c\\mathbin\\square b$ is trivial for every projective trivial cofibration $c$ and every simplicial cofibration $b$. Closure of the resulting left lifting classes follows by the lift extensions in Step 3. This proves the simplicial model axiom for the actual displayed mapping sets of $\\mathcal A^I$ using [lem-coef-simplicial-model-nerve-localization]. Evaluation preserves cofibrations and trivial cofibrations by Step 4, and fibrations by their definition. Hence a projectively cofibrant fibrant diagram has cofibrant fibrant values, since initial and terminal diagrams are pointwise initial and terminal.",
   "uses": [
    "lem-coef-simplicial-model-nerve-localization"
   ],
   "elementary": false,
   "source": {
    "document": "literature/blueprint/main-20261015/blueprint/sec-six-functors/sk-thm-sf-package/prop-coef-integral-exceptional-adjoint.proof.md",
    "place": "Proof paragraph 2, coherent derived coefficient-adjunction foundation; HTT A.3.3.4-A.3.3.5: tensor structure, simplicial compatibility and bifibrant evaluation"
   }
  },
  {
   "text": "For any simplicial functor $v:I\\to J$, define\n$$ (v_!P)_j=\\int^{i\\in I}J(vi,j)\\otimes P_i. $$\nConcretely this is the coequalizer of the two action maps from $\\coprod_{i,i'}(J(vi',j)\\times I(i,i'))\\otimes P_i$ to $\\coprod_iJ(vi,j)\\otimes P_i$. Smallness of $I$ and cocompleteness of $\\mathcal A$ guarantee its existence. The action of $J$ is induced by composition on its first tensor factor. A transformation from this coequalizer to $Q$ is exactly a family $P_i\\to Q_{vi}$ compatible with the $I$-actions: in one direction restrict to the identity of $vi$, and in the other use the action $J(vi,j)\\otimes Q_{vi}\\to Q_j$. The coequalizer relation is precisely the compatibility needed. Tensoring this coequalizer with a parameter $K$ commutes with its coproducts and coequalizer and replaces each $P_i$ by $K\\otimes P_i$. The identity-vertex and action constructions above then give inverse bijections between maps $K\\otimes v_!P\\to Q$ and maps $K\\otimes P\\to v^*Q$. Taking $K=\\Delta^n$ gives bijections on every degree of the enriched mapping sets; their formulas commute with all simplex operators. Thus they give the simplicial adjunction $v_!\\dashv v^*$. Restriction preserves objectwise fibrations and trivial fibrations, so its left adjoint preserves cofibrations and trivial cofibrations by the adjunction of lifting squares. We have a simplicial Quillen adjunction. To identify $v_!F_iX$, maps from it to any $Q$ correspond, by the two adjunctions, to maps $X\\to Q_{vi}$, which correspond to maps $F_{vi}X\\to Q$. These natural bijections identify $v_!F_iX$ with $F_{vi}X$, compatibly with the identity-vertex units. These arguments only use tensor adjunctions and the model lifting definitions in [lem-coef-simplicial-model-nerve-localization].",
   "uses": [
    "lem-coef-simplicial-model-nerve-localization"
   ],
   "elementary": false,
   "source": {
    "document": "literature/blueprint/main-20261015/blueprint/sec-six-functors/sk-thm-sf-package/prop-coef-integral-exceptional-adjoint.proof.md",
    "place": "Proof paragraph 2, coherent derived coefficient-adjunction foundation; HTT A.3.3.7: explicit enriched left Kan extension and its Quillen property"
   }
  },
  {
   "text": "First suppose that $v$ is a DK equivalence and that every $I(i,k)\\to J(vi,vk)$ is degreewise injective. Put $H=v^*v_!$ and let $\\eta:1\\to H$ be its unit; $H$ preserves all colimits by Step 1 and the left adjunction in Step 7. Call $a:P\\to P'$ good when its relative unit\n$$ r_a: HP\\amalg_P P'\\longrightarrow HP' $$\nis objectwise a trivial cofibration. For $a=F_i(s)$, where $s:A\\to B$ is a generating cofibration of $\\mathcal A$, evaluation at $k$ identifies $r_a$ with\n$$ (J(vi,vk)\\otimes A)\\amalg_{I(i,k)\\otimes A}(I(i,k)\\otimes B)\\longrightarrow J(vi,vk)\\otimes B. $$\nThis is the pushout product of $s$ with $I(i,k)\\to J(vi,vk)$. The second factor is a weak equivalence by DK fully faithfulness and a cofibration by the extra injectivity assumption and [lem-coef-kan-quillen-right-proper]. The simplicial model axiom [lem-coef-simplicial-model-nerve-localization] therefore makes this displayed map a trivial cofibration, without any cofibrancy requirement on $A$ or $B$. Every generating projective cofibration is good.",
   "uses": [
    "lem-coef-kan-quillen-right-proper",
    "lem-coef-simplicial-model-nerve-localization"
   ],
   "elementary": false,
   "source": {
    "document": "literature/blueprint/main-20261015/blueprint/sec-six-functors/sk-thm-sf-package/prop-coef-integral-exceptional-adjoint.proof.md",
    "place": "Proof paragraph 2, coherent derived coefficient-adjunction foundation; HTT A.3.3.8, condition (*) and the displayed pushout-product map"
   }
  },
  {
   "text": "The good maps of Step 8 are closed under pushout: if $a'$ is a pushout of $a$, then $r_{a'}$ is the pushout of $r_a$, because $H$ preserves pushouts. They are closed under coproducts and retracts for the same reason. To see the transfinite assertion without presuming left properness, let $(P_\\alpha)_{\\alpha\\leq\\lambda}$ be a continuous sequence of good successor maps, and set $Z_\\alpha=HP_\\alpha\\amalg_{P_\\alpha}P_\\lambda$. For each successor $\\alpha+1$, the map $Z_\\alpha\\to Z_{\\alpha+1}$ is a pushout of $r_{P_\\alpha\\to P_{\\alpha+1}}$; at a limit ordinal the sequence is continuous since $H$ preserves colimits. Thus $Z_0\\to Z_\\lambda=HP_\\lambda$ is a transfinite composite of objectwise trivial cofibrations, and it is exactly $r_{P_0\\to P_\\lambda}$. Such composites remain trivial cofibrations by the successive lifting construction against fibrations, with the lift at a limit supplied by the colimit. This construction uses just the weak factorization systems in [lem-coef-simplicial-model-nerve-localization]. By the cellular-retract description in Step 3 every projective cofibration is good. For a cofibrant $P$ apply this to $0\\to P$: since $H0=0$, its relative unit is $\\eta_P:P\\to v^*v_!P$, hence this unit is an objectwise trivial cofibration.",
   "uses": [
    "lem-coef-simplicial-model-nerve-localization"
   ],
   "elementary": false,
   "source": {
    "document": "literature/blueprint/main-20261015/blueprint/sec-six-functors/sk-thm-sf-package/prop-coef-integral-exceptional-adjoint.proof.md",
    "place": "Proof paragraph 2, coherent derived coefficient-adjunction foundation; HTT A.3.3.8: the weak saturation of good maps, with the continuous transfinite filtration written out"
   }
  },
  {
   "text": "Restriction along a functor essentially surjective on $\\pi_0$ reflects weak equivalences between objectwise fibrant diagrams. Here is the necessary verification. For a fibrant object $Y$ of $\\mathcal A$, the simplicial model axiom makes both evaluations $Y^{\\Delta^1}\\to Y$ trivial fibrations, because either endpoint inclusion $\\Delta^0\\to\\Delta^1$ is a trivial Kan cofibration. Their common constant-path section is consequently a weak equivalence. In the ordinary localization of $\\mathcal A$, these two evaluations have the same inverse and are equal. Thus two maps connected by a simplicial edge in their mapping set have the same localized image when their target is fibrant; a finite zigzag of edges has the same consequence. If $e:j\\to j'$ is invertible in $\\pi_0J$, choose a vertex representing its inverse. Its two composite classes are the identities, so each fibrant-valued enriched diagram carries $e$ to an isomorphism after localization. By [lem-coef-model-localization-detects-weak-equivalences] its image is a weak equivalence. For a transformation $a:Q\\to Q'$ of fibrant-valued diagrams and $j\\in J$, choose an isomorphism in $\\pi_0J$ between $vi$ and $j$ and a representing vertex. Naturality gives the square with horizontal maps $a_{vi},a_j$ and vertical maps just shown to be weak equivalences. If $v^*a$ is an objectwise weak equivalence, two-out-of-three in this square proves that $a_j$ is one. Only the Kan model of [lem-coef-kan-quillen-right-proper], the simplicial model axiom, and localization detection have been used; $I,J$ need not be locally Kan.",
   "uses": [
    "lem-coef-simplicial-model-nerve-localization",
    "lem-coef-kan-quillen-right-proper",
    "lem-coef-model-localization-detects-weak-equivalences"
   ],
   "elementary": false,
   "source": {
    "document": "literature/blueprint/main-20261015/blueprint/sec-six-functors/sk-thm-sf-package/prop-coef-integral-exceptional-adjoint.proof.md",
    "place": "Proof paragraph 2, coherent derived coefficient-adjunction foundation; HTT A.3.3.8: essential-surjectivity detection, with fibrant-value and path-object hypotheses made explicit"
   }
  },
  {
   "text": "Apply [lem-coef-model-infinity-derived-adjunction] to Step 7 and take $\\pi_0$ of its natural mapping-space equivalences, obtaining an ordinary derived adjunction on homotopy categories. These homotopy categories are ordinary localizations at the model weak equivalences: test the infinity-localization universal property on the nerve of an ordinary category, for which functors factor through the homotopy category. Restriction preserves all weak equivalences, so its derived functor is its ordinary localized functor. For the mappingwise injective DK equivalence considered in Steps 8-9, the derived unit at a cofibrant $P$ is $P\\xrightarrow{\\eta_P}v^*v_!P\\to v^*R(v_!P)$; both maps are weak equivalences, the first by Step 9 and the second because restriction preserves weak equivalences. Thus the derived unit is invertible at every object, since every object has a cofibrant replacement. The derived restriction reflects isomorphisms: represent an arbitrary morphism between bifibrant diagram representatives by an actual map, using [lem-coef-model-localization-homotopy-classes]; if its restriction is invertible after localization, [lem-coef-model-localization-detects-weak-equivalences] makes its restricted representative a weak equivalence, Step 10 reflects that weak equivalence, and localization inverts it. The derived triangle identity now implies that the restriction of each derived counit is inverse to the corresponding derived unit; reflection makes the counit itself invertible. Therefore the two derived functors are inverse equivalences for every mappingwise injective DK equivalence.",
   "uses": [
    "lem-coef-model-infinity-derived-adjunction",
    "lem-coef-model-localization-homotopy-classes",
    "lem-coef-model-localization-detects-weak-equivalences"
   ],
   "elementary": false,
   "source": {
    "document": "literature/blueprint/main-20261015/blueprint/sec-six-functors/sk-thm-sf-package/prop-coef-integral-exceptional-adjoint.proof.md",
    "place": "Proof paragraph 2, coherent derived coefficient-adjunction foundation; HTT A.3.3.8 under (*): derived unit, reflection and counit, without an implicit equivalence-detection assertion"
   }
  },
  {
   "text": "Reduce an arbitrary DK equivalence $v:I\\to J$ to the case just proved, using [lem-coef-fixed-object-simplicial-free-calculus]. If $I$ is empty, essential surjectivity forces $J$ to be empty and both diagram categories are terminal, so all assertions hold. Otherwise let $O=\\operatorname{Ob}I\\amalg\\operatorname{Ob}J$. In the fixed-object model category $\\mathrm{sCat}_O$, factor the map from the disjoint union $I\\amalg J$ to the category having mapping set $\\Delta^0$ between every pair of elements of $O$ as a cofibration $I\\amalg J\\to D$ followed by a trivial fibration. The latter is a trivial Kan fibration on every mapping set, so all $D(x,y)$ are nonempty contractible Kan complexes. The cofibration is mappingwise monic: by the cited node it is a retract, with fixed domain, of a degreewise free extension; the free-map statement explicitly supplies degreewise inclusions on mapping sets, and a retract of an injection is injective (if two source elements have the same image, apply the arrow-retract inclusion, use injectivity there, then the retraction). Write $d_I:I\\to D$ and $d_J:J\\to D$ for its restrictions. Set $K=J\\times D$, $p:K\\to J$, $g=(v,d_I):I\\to K$, and $s=(1_J,d_J):J\\to K$. Every mapping map of $p$ is a trivial Kan fibration, being the pullback of $D(x,y)\\to\\Delta^0$; on $\\pi_0$, $p$ is fully faithful and essentially surjective since $O$ is nonempty and $\\pi_0D$ has one arrow between any two objects. Hence $p$ is a DK equivalence. The identities $pg=v$ and $ps=1_J$ show that $g,s$ are DK equivalences as well (on mapping sets by two-out-of-three; on $\\pi_0$ because a composite and one factor are equivalences). Their mapping maps are monomorphisms: for $g$ its second coordinate is injective, and for $s$ its first coordinate is the identity. This supplies exactly the reduction of HTT A.3.3.8 without imposing local Kan conditions on the original $I$ or $J$. The Kan properties used here are those of [lem-coef-kan-quillen-right-proper], and DK terminology is [lem-coef-coherent-nerve-comparison].",
   "uses": [
    "lem-coef-fixed-object-simplicial-free-calculus",
    "lem-coef-kan-quillen-right-proper",
    "lem-coef-coherent-nerve-comparison"
   ],
   "elementary": false,
   "source": {
    "document": "literature/blueprint/main-20261015/blueprint/sec-six-functors/sk-thm-sf-package/prop-coef-integral-exceptional-adjoint.proof.md",
    "place": "Proof paragraph 2, coherent derived coefficient-adjunction foundation; HTT A.3.3.8 after (*): factor through a contractible auxiliary simplicial category; fixed-object factorization supplies the locally monic inclusions"
   }
  },
  {
   "text": "Step 11 applies to $g$ and $s$ of Step 12. At the level of homotopy categories, the localized restriction $s^*$ is therefore an equivalence, and $s^*p^*=1$. It follows that $p^*$ is its inverse equivalence: apply a quasi-inverse of $s^*$ to this identity to obtain $p^*\\cong(s^*)^{-1}$. Consequently $v^*=g^*p^*$ is an equivalence. The derived adjunction supplied by [lem-coef-model-infinity-derived-adjunction] has this functor as its right adjoint; its unit and counit are invertible. Explicitly, writing $L=\\mathbf L v_!$ and $U=\\mathbf Rv^*$, full faithfulness of $U$ identifies the adjunction bijection $\\operatorname{Hom}(LUY,Z)\\cong\\operatorname{Hom}(UY,UZ)$ with $\\operatorname{Hom}(LUY,Z)\\cong\\operatorname{Hom}(Y,Z)$, whose inverse is precomposition with the counit $\\epsilon_Y$. Taking $Z=LUY$ and $Z=Y$ shows that $\\epsilon_Y$ has a two-sided inverse. The triangle identity $U\\epsilon_Y\\circ\\eta_{UY}=1$ then makes $\\eta_{UY}$ invertible; essential surjectivity of $U$ and naturality of $\\eta$ make every unit component invertible. Hence $\\mathbf L v_!$ is its inverse. Finally, for cofibrant $P$ and fibrant $Q$, the derived adjunction identifies the class of an actual map $a:v_!P\\to Q$ with the class of its ordinary adjunct $\\bar a:P\\to v^*Q$, using the replacement identifications of that cited node. The identity $[\\bar a]=(\\mathbf Rv^*)[a]\\circ\\eta_P$ has invertible $\\eta_P$, and $\\mathbf Rv^*$ is an equivalence, so $[a]$ is invertible if and only if $[\\bar a]$ is invertible. Apply [lem-coef-model-localization-detects-weak-equivalences] in the two diagram model categories to translate this equivalence into exactly the weak-equivalence criterion in the item. Together with Steps 1-7 this proves every asserted clause.",
   "uses": [
    "lem-coef-model-infinity-derived-adjunction",
    "lem-coef-model-localization-detects-weak-equivalences"
   ],
   "elementary": false,
